.TH "splitlayout" 3tcl 0\&.1 "splitlayout" "splitlayout package"
.SH NAME
splitlayout - Commands in namespace splitlayout
.SH SYNOPSIS
\fBsplitlayout create OBJNAME\fP \fIpath ?-width width? ?-height height? ?-opaqueresize boolean? ?-sashpreview mode? ?-draghandles boolean? ?-buttonside side? ?-showstructure boolean? ?-dockopts dictionary? ?-structureopts dictionary? ?-sashpreviewopts dictionary? ?-leafborder boolean? ?-leafborderwidth pixels? ?-leafrelief relief? ?-leafopts dictionary? ?-headerrelief relief? ?-headerborderwidth pixels? ?-tooltips boolean? ?-titlealign alignment? ?-buttonimages dictionary? ?-lockbuttonmode mode?\fP
.br
\fBsplitlayout new\fP \fIpath ?-width width? ?-height height? ?-opaqueresize boolean? ?-sashpreview mode? ?-draghandles boolean? ?-buttonside side? ?-showstructure boolean? ?-dockopts dictionary? ?-structureopts dictionary? ?-sashpreviewopts dictionary? ?-leafborder boolean? ?-leafborderwidth pixels? ?-leafrelief relief? ?-leafopts dictionary? ?-headerrelief relief? ?-headerborderwidth pixels? ?-tooltips boolean? ?-titlealign alignment? ?-buttonimages dictionary? ?-lockbuttonmode mode?\fP
.br
\fBSPLITLAYOUTOBJ destroy\fP
.br
\fBSPLITLAYOUTOBJ children\fP \fIid\fP
.br
\fBSPLITLAYOUTOBJ clear\fP
.br
\fBSPLITLAYOUTOBJ constraints\fP \fIid ?option? | id ?-nomove boolean? ?-noundock boolean? ?-nodestroy boolean? ?-noresize boolean? ?-locked boolean? ?-lockedside sides? ?-minleaves count? ?-maxleaves count? ?-maxdepth depth?\fP
.br
\fBSPLITLAYOUTOBJ container\fP \fIvalue\fP
.br
\fBSPLITLAYOUTOBJ dock\fP \fIfloating ?-left target|-right target|-top target|-bottom target|-before target|-after target?\fP
.br
\fBSPLITLAYOUTOBJ floating\fP \fI?id?\fP
.br
\fBSPLITLAYOUTOBJ frame\fP \fIid\fP
.br
\fBSPLITLAYOUTOBJ insert\fP \fInode ?-side side?\fP
.br
\fBSPLITLAYOUTOBJ leaves\fP \fI?id?\fP
.br
\fBSPLITLAYOUTOBJ locate\fP \fIpath\fP
.br
\fBSPLITLAYOUTOBJ minsize\fP \fIid ?width height?\fP
.br
\fBSPLITLAYOUTOBJ move\fP \fIleaf -left|-right|-top|-bottom|-before|-after target\fP
.br
\fBSPLITLAYOUTOBJ node\fP \fIpath\fP
.br
\fBSPLITLAYOUTOBJ panedwindow\fP \fIid\fP
.br
\fBSPLITLAYOUTOBJ parent\fP \fIid\fP
.br
\fBSPLITLAYOUTOBJ proportions\fP \fInode ?weights?\fP
.br
\fBSPLITLAYOUTOBJ ratio\fP \fInode ?value?\fP
.br
\fBSPLITLAYOUTOBJ remove\fP \fIid\fP
.br
\fBSPLITLAYOUTOBJ requiredsize\fP \fIid\fP
.br
\fBSPLITLAYOUTOBJ retain\fP \fIid\fP
.br
\fBSPLITLAYOUTOBJ root\fP
.br
\fBSPLITLAYOUTOBJ split\fP \fInode ?-orient orientation? ?-ratio fraction? ?-keep child?\fP
.br
\fBSPLITLAYOUTOBJ swap\fP \fIa b\fP
.br
\fBSPLITLAYOUTOBJ title\fP \fIid ?text?\fP
.br
\fBSPLITLAYOUTOBJ tree\fP \fI?id?\fP
.br
\fBSPLITLAYOUTOBJ type\fP \fIid\fP
.br
\fBSPLITLAYOUTOBJ undock\fP \fIleaf ?-title title? ?-geometry geometry? ?-onclose action?\fP
.br
\fBSPLITLAYOUTOBJ widget\fP \fIid\fP
.br
\fBSPLITLAYOUTOBJ widgets\fP \fI?path?\fP
.br
.SH SPLITLAYOUT
.SH "QUICK START"
.PP
.RS 4n
.nf
package require splitlayout

wm title \&. {Split layout example}
wm geometry \&. 900x600

splitlayout \&.layout -width 900 -height 600
pack \&.layout -fill both -expand 1

set root [\&.layout root]
lassign [\&.layout split $root -orient horizontal -ratio 0\&.4 -keep first] left right

set leftFrame [\&.layout frame $left]
set rightFrame [\&.layout frame $right]

text $leftFrame\&.editor -width 20 -height 10 -wrap word
$leftFrame\&.editor insert end {Content in the left pane\&.}
pack $leftFrame\&.editor -fill both -expand 1

text $rightFrame\&.editor -width 20 -height 10 -wrap word
$rightFrame\&.editor insert end {Content in the right pane\&. Drag the sash to resize\&.}
pack $rightFrame\&.editor -fill both -expand 1
.fi
.RE
.PP
The splitlayout \&.layout \&.\&.\&. command returns the widget pathname\&. Use that pathname both as the layout command
and with Tk geometry managers\&. The constructor accepts -width, -height, -opaqueresize, -sashpreview,
-draghandles, -buttonside, -showstructure, -dockopts, -structureopts, and -sashpreviewopts\&.
.SH "SPLITTING EXISTING CONTENT"
.PP
Continue from the quick-start example:
.PP
.RS 4n
.nf
lassign [\&.layout split $right -orient vertical -ratio 0\&.65 -keep first] top bottom

set bottomFrame [\&.layout frame $bottom]
ttk::label $bottomFrame\&.status -text {New bottom pane}
pack $bottomFrame\&.status -fill both -expand 1
.fi
.RE
.PP
The original right-hand editor remains in $top, with the same widget pathname and state\&. $right now
identifies the parent split; $top and $bottom are new leaf identifiers\&. -keep second would retain the
editor in the second child instead\&. Both children are returned in pane order\&.
.SH "ADDING PANES AT THE SAME LEVEL"
.PP
Insert beside a leaf or a split, provided that it has a parent:
.PP
.RS 4n
.nf
set middle [\&.layout insert $left -side after]
set middleFrame [\&.layout frame $middle]
ttk::label $middleFrame\&.label -text {Middle pane}
pack $middleFrame\&.label -fill both -expand 1

# The root now has three children: left, middle, and the right-hand split\&.
\&.layout proportions $root {2 1 3}
.fi
.RE
.PP
Insertion divides the target child's existing share equally between that child and the new leaf\&. The -side
option accepts before or after, with after as the default\&. To add panes when the root is still a leaf,
split it first\&.
.PP
proportions accepts one finite positive weight per immediate child and normalizes the weights\&. The example
assigns shares of 2/6, 1/6, and 3/6\&. For a split with exactly two children, ratio queries or sets the first
child's fraction:
.PP
.RS 4n
.nf
\&.layout ratio $right 0\&.7
set fraction [\&.layout ratio $right]
set shares [\&.layout proportions $root]
.fi
.RE
.PP
Geometry changes settle through Tk's event loop\&. Immediately after setting proportions, a query can return the
stored values before the sashes have moved\&.
.SH "MINIMUM CONTENT SIZES"
.PP
.RS 4n
.nf
$manager minsize $leaf 200 120
puts [$manager minsize $leaf]                ;# 200 120 (content area)
puts [$manager requiredsize [$manager root]] ;# Recursive outer minimum
$manager minsize $leaf 0 0                   ;# Clear the content-area minimum
.fi
.RE
.PP
minsize id ?width height? accepts a leaf or an owned floating token\&. Both dimensions must be supplied together
as nonnegative integer pixels; the default is {0 0}\&. Splits have calculated requirements, not independently
assignable minima\&. Values belong to content: they follow swaps, moves, the child selected by split -keep, and
undocking/redocking\&. A newly created leaf starts at {0 0}\&. Removing content removes its stored minimum\&.
.PP
requiredsize id accepts a leaf, split, or floating token and returns {width height}\&. It adds header/button
space and leaf borders to the content minimum\&. For a horizontal split, child widths and themed sash widths are
summed and the largest child height is used\&. Vertical splits use the opposite axes\&. Each split's structure
border and padding are added when enabled\&. Removing, inserting, or moving children updates the calculated
requirement\&.  The query excludes the manager hull's padding/border, native window decorations, and application
widget requests\&.  It works before mapping; sash dimensions come from the active ttk theme without modifying its
styles\&.
.PP
With explicit minima configured, requested proportions are fitted to available space while respecting child
requirements\&. Both live and deferred sash gestures stop at the adjacent panes' feasible limits\&. When the parent
is too small, child requirements along each split axis are compressed proportionally; requested minima are
retained and apply again when space becomes available\&. A cross-axis shortage likewise cannot satisfy the
requested minimum\&.  Very small allocations may leave no visible content after sashes and decoration consume the
available pixels\&.  Setting {0 0} clears the content requirement; enabled headers and borders still require
space\&.  Layouts that have never been assigned minima retain their existing sash behavior\&.
.PP
Floating windows receive wm minsize based on their content and decoration\&. Header visibility, image sizes,
border settings, and theme changes refresh constraints through the normal idle geometry pass\&. The manager does
\fBnot\fP set wm minsize on the application's owning toplevel: applications can use requiredsize and account
for their other widgets if they want to enforce a window minimum\&. Native window-manager behavior remains
platform-dependent\&. Tk may report a larger minimum than requested because of native window limits\&. In
particular, Windows can report a positive minimum width after setting wm minsize to 0 0, and its reported
limits can change when a window is first mapped\&.  requiredsize reports the manager's calculated requirement,
not the native effective window minimum\&.  Constraints cover manager operations and gestures; direct
modifications through the exposed native panedwindow command bypass the manager until its next geometry
application\&.
.SH "PER-NODE CONSTRAINTS"
.PP
Use constraints id to query explicit settings, constraints id option to query one value, and constraints
id option value \&.\&.\&. to change several settings atomically\&. A floating token is accepted for content
flags\&. Constraints are independent between manager instances, including nested managers\&.
.PP
.RS 4n
.nf
lassign [$manager split [$manager root]] editor output
$manager constraints $editor -locked true -nodestroy true
$manager constraints [$manager root] -minleaves 2 -maxleaves 6 -maxdepth 3
puts [$manager constraints $editor -nomove]
$manager constraints $output -noresize true
.fi
.RE
.PP
.RS 4n
.nf
┌─────────────┬─────────────────────────┬──────────────────────────┬───────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
│ Option      │ Default                 │ Applies to               │ Meaning                                           │
├─────────────┼─────────────────────────┼──────────────────────────┼───────────────────────────────────────────────────┤
│ -nomove     │ false                   │ Leaf / floating content  │ Reject direct move and either operand of swap\&.    │
│ -noundock   │ false                   │ Leaf / floating content  │ Reject undocking this content\&.                    │
│ -nodestroy  │ false                   │ Any node / floating      │ Reject deletion of protected content or an        │
│             │                         │ content                  │ explicitly protected split\&.                       │
│ -noresize   │ false                   │ Any node / floating      │ Lock sash-driven allocation changes; this is not  │
│             │                         │ content                  │ a fixed pixel size\&.                               │
│ -locked     │ false                   │ Leaf / floating content  │ Set movement flags and block adjacent move/dock   │
│             │                         │                          │ destinations\&.                                     │
│ -lockedside │ {left right top bottom} │ Leaf / floating content  │ Edges blocking incoming move/dock placement while │
│             │                         │                          │ locked\&.                                           │
│ -minleaves  │ 1                       │ Docked node              │ Minimum descendant leaf count while this subtree  │
│             │                         │                          │ is retained\&.                                      │
│ -maxleaves  │ -1                      │ Docked node              │ Maximum descendant leaf count; -1 means           │
│             │                         │                          │ unlimited\&.                                        │
│ -maxdepth   │ -1                      │ Docked node              │ Maximum depth below the node; -1 means unlimited\&. │
└─────────────┴─────────────────────────┴──────────────────────────┴───────────────────────────────────────────────────┘
.fi
.RE
.SS "LOCK TERMINOLOGY AND MIGRATION"
.PP
Locking replaces the former pin terminology throughout the API and user interface\&. Update existing scripts
and custom image dictionaries using these names; the former names are not retained as aliases:
.PP
.RS 4n
.nf
┌─────────────────┬──────────────────┐
│ Former name     │ Current name     │
├─────────────────┼──────────────────┤
│ -pinned         │ -locked          │
│ -pinnedside     │ -lockedside      │
│ -pinbuttonmode  │ -lockbuttonmode  │
│ Image key pin   │ Image key lock   │
│ Image key unpin │ Image key unlock │
└─────────────────┴──────────────────┘
.fi
.RE
.PP
The all/outer modes, side lists, constraint behavior, root-edge propagation, and interaction with
-nomove and -noundock are unchanged\&. Constraint errors now identify -locked when locking blocks placement\&.
.SS "MOVEMENT AND LOCKED CONTENT"
.PP
-nomove checks only the directly moved leaf\&. It is not imposed on ancestors\&. In a swap, both existing flags
are checked because both contents would move; no flag is added to the destination\&. Moving another leaf beside
protected content is allowed, including converting its current leaf position into a split\&. Moving or wrapping
an ancestor, changing neighboring panes, and normal geometry changes can change a protected leaf's screen
position\&.
.PP
-nomove alone does not prevent undocking, docking, deletion, splitting, resizing, or moving a floating window
on screen\&. -noundock is the separate restriction on leaving the docked tree\&. It does not prohibit returning
floating content with dock, or moving and swapping it while docked\&. The current public move, swap, and
undock commands accept leaves, not complete split subtrees; movement flags cannot be assigned to split nodes\&.
.PP
-locked true sets both movement flags and protects adjacent placement boundaries\&. move and dock,
including gestures, reject placing content against the edges selected by -lockedside\&. Shared slots check both
neighbors: naming the unlocked neighbor with -before or -after cannot bypass the restriction\&. Sash and
outer-rim drop zones use the same validation\&. Internal ancestor sashes check only their direct leaf
neighbors: locking the bottom leaf of a vertical middle split does not block the outer vertical sashes beside
that split\&. Its own selected edges and adjacent sibling slots remain protected, and a directly neighboring
locked leaf can still block an internal slot\&.
.PP
There is one exception: an insertion boundary coinciding with an outer root edge also checks descendants
touching that edge\&. For example, a nested bottom leaf blocking bottom blocks placement below the whole
root, or below its containing split when that split reaches the root's bottom edge\&. Its left/right settings
do not block internal ancestor sashes\&. Descendants that do not touch the selected root edge do not restrict
it\&. The rule uses the proposed tree after removing a moving source, works before mapping, and applies to
commands, gestures, outer-rim drops, and remembered docking alike\&. It never changes stored flags or side lists\&.
.PP
-lockedside is a list of blocked physical edges: left, right, top, and bottom\&. The default is
{left right top bottom}\&. For example, leave the right edge available so other floating windows can return
even when this is the only remaining docked leaf:
.PP
.RS 4n
.nf
$manager constraints $editor -locked true -lockedside {left top bottom}
$manager dock $floatingToken -right $editor
.fi
.RE
.PP
Both sides of a shared slot are checked against each neighbor's own physical edge: inserting after a leaf
checks its right edge in a horizontal parent and its bottom edge in a vertical parent\&. The opposite
neighbor's left or top edge is checked too\&. Before/after are destination commands, not valid lockedside
values\&. Split neighbors are searched for locked descendants only at coincident outer root edges\&.
.PP
An empty list allows incoming placement on every edge while keeping the locked leaf's movement and undocking
restrictions\&. Setting -lockedside alone does not enable locking; it can be configured in advance, and it is
preserved when locking is switched off and on\&. Queries return a unique list in left/right/top/bottom order\&.
The setting belongs to leaf/floating content, follows retained splits and docking, and cannot be set on a split\&.
Invalid lists reject the whole update without changing settings or cancelling active gestures\&.
.PP
Default docking still tries its remembered destination (or the existing fallback); it does not search for
an unblocked edge\&. If that destination is blocked, use an explicit open edge such as dock -right, or drag
to that edge\&. Buttons and native close requests use the same default-destination validation as before\&.
.PP
Locked is stored explicitly\&. Setting -nomove true -noundock true separately does not lock destinations\&.
-locked false clears locking and both movement flags without changing other restrictions\&. Clearing either
movement flag individually also clears locking, leaving the other flag unchanged\&. Set -locked in a separate
call from -nomove or -noundock; mixing them in one update remains an error\&. Existing no-op moves are
allowed\&.
.PP
Locking does not block explicit creation through split or insert, deletion, sash resizing, or ordinary
geometry changes\&. Use -nodestroy, -noresize, and subtree limits for those separate restrictions\&. The flag
follows retained content through splitting and docking just like the other leaf flags\&. A floating locked
frame may be docked at an allowed destination; once docked, its adjacent boundaries are protected\&.
.SS "DESTRUCTION AND COLLAPSE"
.PP
-nodestroy on leaf content rejects remove, clear, and retain when they would delete that content, even
through an ancestor\&. It does not prevent undocking or splitting with retained content, because the frame
survives\&. Removing an unprotected sibling is allowed, including automatic collapse of an unprotected parent
that preserves protected content\&. A split explicitly marked -nodestroy cannot itself be deleted or collapsed;
otherwise its unprotected descendants may still be edited or removed while the split survives\&.
.PP
Ancestor flags are not rewritten\&. An ancestor can report -nodestroy false while a descendant makes its
removal illegal\&. Validation considers everything the whole operation would delete before changing any widgets
or records\&.  Delete buttons obey these restrictions\&. Native close with -onclose destroy leaves protected
content open; -onclose dock attempts normal docking and likewise leaves the window open if the destination
violates constraints\&.
.PP
These are manager policies, not an access-control boundary\&. $manager destroy, direct Tcl destroy,
destruction of an actual Tk ancestor, and application shutdown remain possible\&. Removing an outer manager's
content also destroys nested managers inside it; their independent policies cannot veto their actual Tk
ancestor's destruction\&. Raw Tk commands obtained through frame, widget, or panedwindow can bypass manager
policies\&.
.SS "RESIZE LOCKS"
.PP
-noresize rejects sash gestures that would change the allocation of a protected node, including ancestor
sashes\&.  An explicitly locked split can still adjust its own internal sashes if its children are not
locked\&. With resize locks present, a sash cannot push neighboring sashes to resize a more distant protected
pane\&. Changing constraints cancels active manager gestures and prevents further movement from an already active
native live sash gesture\&.
.PP
Changed ratio and proportions settings are conservatively rejected if the configured split contains a
locked descendant: minimum-size fitting can redistribute space beyond the requested panes\&. Reapplying the
identical stored proportions is allowed\&. A lock on the split itself does not prohibit its internal proportion
changes\&. Unrelated subtrees remain resizable\&.
.PP
This does not freeze an absolute size or enforce wm resizable: resizing the application or a floating window,
changing minimum sizes or decoration, and structural operations such as split, insert, move, swap, and dock may
resize the content\&. A floating content flag takes effect on its sash allocation after docking\&. Use minsize for
minimum dimensions; the manager must still compress allocations when its parent supplies insufficient space\&.
.SS "LEAF COUNTS AND DEPTH"
.PP
A leaf counts as one and has depth zero\&. A split containing only leaves has depth one; nesting adds one per
edge\&.  Thus -maxdepth 0 prevents splitting that node or converting it into a split by moving or docking
another leaf at its edge\&. Counts include all docked descendant leaves, not only immediate children\&. Floating
content is excluded\&.  Minimum counts are nonnegative integers; maxima accept nonnegative integers or -1\&. The
layout always has at least one root leaf, including its empty placeholder\&. All numeric constraint values are
limited to 2147483647\&.
.PP
The complete final layout is checked after planned insertion, source removal, and automatic collapse\&. Moving
within one bounded subtree does not temporarily violate its count\&. Moving between subtrees must satisfy source
and destination limits and all affected ancestors\&. Undocking can violate a minimum; docking, splitting, and
insertion can violate a maximum or depth limit\&. Reordering flat siblings does not increase depth\&.
.PP
Automatic collapse cannot evade the old parent's minimum: its surviving replacement subtree is checked against
that parent's limits before collapse is allowed\&. Limits belong to structural node identity and are discarded
when that node expires; they are not transferred to a promoted child\&. Explicit deletion of an entire
constrained subtree (including clear) removes its limits rather than requiring it to remain present\&. Add
-nodestroy to protect the node's existence\&. Surviving ancestors' limits are always checked\&.
.PP
New limits must already be satisfied by the current subtree, and minimum must not exceed a finite
maximum\&. Invalid updates leave all settings and active gestures unchanged; no panes are silently removed or
rearranged to fit\&.
.SS "OWNERSHIP, COMMANDS, AND GESTURE FEEDBACK"
.PP
Leaf flags belong to the content frame and follow allowed swaps, moves, and docking transitions\&. When a leaf is
split, they follow the child selected by -keep; the new empty child has default flags\&. Split flags and
subtree limits belong to node identity\&. Limits assigned to a leaf stay on that identifier when it becomes a
split\&. Leaf IDs expire on undocking, so their structural limits are discarded; the floating content retains its
flags and redocking creates a new node with default limits\&. Floating queries show default subtree-limit values,
but setting those limits requires a docked node\&. Removing content discards its flags\&.
.PP
Commands reject prohibited operations before allocating widgets, changing IDs, cancelling gestures, or mutating
the tree\&. Errors have -errorcode {SPLITLAYOUT CONSTRAINT option id} and identify the restriction that blocked
the action\&.  Identical-node swaps and moves, and already-adjacent moves that make no change, remain
no-ops\&. Drop previews suppress invalid destinations and release does nothing there\&. Header buttons retain their
action icons, including custom images, and become inactive when their current action is illegal\&.  The same
image is assigned explicitly to normal and disabled states; ttk still controls button styling\&.  Blocked native
close requests leave the window open without a background error\&. Flags are explicit settings, not a cached
summary of every action that might be prohibited by descendants or ancestor bounds\&.
.SH "MOVING PANES"
.PP
move relocates an existing leaf while retaining its identifier, content frame, widget pathnames, and
application state\&. Supply exactly one destination option:
.PP
.RS 4n
.nf
┌────────────────┬────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
│ Destination    │ Effect                                                         │
├────────────────┼────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤
│ -left target   │ Place the source to the left of a target leaf or split\&.        │
│ -right target  │ Place the source to the right of a target leaf or split\&.       │
│ -top target    │ Place the source above a target leaf or split\&.                 │
│ -bottom target │ Place the source below a target leaf or split\&.                 │
│ -before target │ Insert before a non-root leaf or split in its existing parent\&. │
│ -after target  │ Insert after a non-root leaf or split in its existing parent\&.  │
└────────────────┴────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘
.fi
.RE
.PP
For example, in the layout built above:
.PP
.RS 4n
.nf
# Insert the middle pane above the bottom pane in the existing vertical split\&.
\&.layout move $middle -top $bottom

# Insert it before the entire right-hand region in the root split\&.
\&.layout move $middle -before $right
.fi
.RE
.PP
Edge destinations reuse the target's parent when it already has the required orientation\&. Otherwise, a target
leaf becomes a new split containing the source and a new leaf for the target's existing content\&. This follows
split's identifier semantics: the target identifier now denotes a split\&. Use children, leaves, or
locate to obtain the new target-content leaf identifier\&. If the target is already a split, a new parent split
is created around the source and target subtree; the target keeps its identifier unless source removal leaves
it with a sole child and normal collapse promotes that child\&. A split target may contain the source, including
the root\&. For example, \&.layout move $source -right [\&.layout root] places the source beside the remaining
layout in a new horizontal root\&.  The source identifier remains unchanged and is also the command's return
value\&.
.PP
For example, a perpendicular move in a two-pane horizontal layout changes it to a vertical layout:
.PP
.RS 4n
.nf
splitlayout \&.example
pack \&.example -fill both -expand 1
lassign [\&.example split [\&.example root] -orient horizontal] a b
set moved [\&.example move $a -top $b]
# moved equals a; b is now the root vertical split\&.
.fi
.RE
.PP
-before and -after always use the existing parent orientation\&. They can describe a sash destination without
pixel coordinates: insertion at a sash is -before its following child or -after its preceding child\&. A
split target may contain the source; moving beside that ancestor moves the source outside its
subtree\&. Insertion beside the root is an error, except that any move to oneself is a no-op\&.
.PP
Reordering within the same parent preserves the pane weights associated with each node\&. Other moves divide the
target's share equally between the source and target\&. Remaining siblings in the source parent retain their
relative weights\&. If only one child remains there, it replaces the old parent after insertion; that old parent
identifier becomes invalid\&. Query root again after a move that can collapse the root\&.
.PP
Moving to oneself or to an already adjacent position with the requested orientation does nothing\&. Invalid
arguments are rejected before modifying the layout\&. A structural move cancels an active deferred sash gesture,
rebuilds geometry once, and lets geometry settle through the normal event loop\&. No application content is
destroyed\&.
.PP
move changes pane placement; swap exchanges content at two existing positions\&.
.SH "DRAGGING PANES"
.PP
Enable minimal, manager-owned grips for every leaf with the global -draghandles property:
.PP
.RS 4n
.nf
splitlayout \&.docking -draghandles true -opaqueresize false
pack \&.docking -fill both -expand 1
lassign [\&.docking split [\&.docking root]] first second

# The existing content API is unchanged\&.
set f [\&.docking frame $first]
text $f\&.editor
pack $f\&.editor -fill both -expand 1

# Hide all grips and reclaim their space, including for leaves created later\&.
\&.docking configure -draghandles false
.fi
.RE
.PP
The default is false\&. When enabled, each leaf has a full-width rectangular header containing an optional
centered title, a dock/undock button, and a delete-content button\&. The grip uses ttk theme colors and a height
scaled to Tk's display scaling\&. Application widgets remain entirely inside the frame returned by frame; pack
and grid configurations there survive toggles and moves\&.
.PP
Press the left mouse button on a grip and move at least five pixels (the default -dockopts -threshold) to
start dragging\&. The strip outside its buttons accepts the press; content widgets retain their own mouse
bindings\&. The destination is selected within this megawidget:
.PP
.RS 4n
.nf
┌────────────────────────────────────────┬─────────────────────────────────────────────────┬───────────────────────────┐
│ Drop location                          │ Operation                                       │ Preview                   │
├────────────────────────────────────────┼─────────────────────────────────────────────────┼───────────────────────────┤
│ Another leaf's center                  │ Swap the two content frames\&.                    │ Outline of the            │
│                                        │                                                 │ destination pane\&.         │
│ Left, right, top, or bottom edge of    │ Move beside that leaf using the corresponding   │ Narrow band at the chosen │
│ another leaf                           │ edge option\&.                                    │ edge\&.                     │
│ Outer rim of the megawidget            │ Move outside the entire layout region on that   │ Full-height or full-width │
│                                        │ side\&.                                           │ edge band\&.                │
│ A native sash between panes            │ Insert before the child following that sash\&.    │ Insertion line at the     │
│                                        │                                                 │ sash\&.                     │
│ Source leaf                            │ Cancel\&.                                         │ No destination highlight\&. │
│ Outside the megawidget, on screen      │ Undock the leaf into a new window\&.              │ Floating window outline\&.  │
└────────────────────────────────────────┴─────────────────────────────────────────────────┴───────────────────────────┘
.fi
.RE
.PP
By default, the outermost 12 pixels \fBinside\fP the megawidget form an outer drop zone (limited to one eighth of
its size)\&. This zone takes precedence over sashes and local leaf edges\&. It allows moving a pane to the far
right or left of an entire stacked region, or above/below an entire row\&. A matching root orientation reuses
that split's first or last position; otherwise a new root split is created\&. Drop just inside the window: points
outside the megawidget undock on release\&.
.PP
Farther inside, sashes take precedence over leaf edge zones\&. Local edge zones default to up to 24 pixels into a
pane, limited to one quarter of its width or height; corners choose the nearest normalized edge\&. The preview is
blue by default, is temporary, and does not resize or rearrange content during dragging\&. Release performs one
move, swap, undock, or dock; a click without dragging does nothing\&. The -sashpreview setting
controls deferred sash resizing only, not this docking preview\&.
.PP
Escape, loss of grip focus, hiding or resizing panes, moving or hiding the containing window, structural layout
changes, or disabling -draghandles cancels the gesture\&. The grip temporarily takes keyboard focus and a local
mouse grab so that release outside the grip and Escape are handled\&. Cleanup restores the prior focus when still
owned by the grip and releases only its own grab\&. A new gesture does not replace an existing grab\&. Dragging
into separate splitlayout instances or other applications is not supported; an outside drop creates an owned
floating window instead\&.
.PP
With handles enabled, widget leaf returns the \fBpane wrapper\fP, whereas frame leaf always returns the
application content frame\&. container leaf resolves the wrapper's outer geometry container; container
$contentFrame resolves the wrapper that packs the content\&. node recognizes either pathname\&. locate
resolves application widgets and manager-owned grips to their current leaf\&. Logical tree snapshots expose
both widget and frame; actual widgets snapshots also include the wrappers, grip canvases, and any
temporary preview frames\&.
.PP
The wrapper and grip stay at their leaf position during a swap; only content frames are exchanged\&. A move
preserves the moved leaf and its wrapper, while normal split/collapse rules may replace destination
wrappers\&. Disabling handles removes wrappers unless leaf borders still need them; do not cache wrapper or
grip paths across layout changes\&. Application content paths remain stable\&.
.PP
Floating windows also have a full-width internal grip when -draghandles is enabled\&. Drag this grip to move
the floating window and preview destinations in its owner layout\&. Drop on an edge, sash, or outer rim to dock
there\&. A floating drop in a leaf's center docks to its right (it does not swap or discard that leaf)\&. Its
untouched sole-root placeholder is filled instead\&. Release elsewhere keeps the window floating at its new
position\&. Escape cancels and restores the starting position\&. Moving native window decorations only moves the
window; it does not initiate docking\&.
.PP
Each grip has three monochrome bitmap buttons, with tooltips:
.PP
.RS 4n
.nf
┌────────┬────────────────────────────┬─────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
│ Button │ Docked leaf                │ Floating content                                │
├────────┼────────────────────────────┼─────────────────────────────────────────────────┤
│ Lock   │ Toggle locking             │ Toggle locking                                  │
│ Arrow  │ Undock                     │ Dock at the default destination defined by dock │
│ Cross  │ Delete content with remove │ Delete content with remove                      │
└────────┴────────────────────────────┴─────────────────────────────────────────────────┘
.fi
.RE
.PP
The cross requests content deletion, subject to constraints\&. It never consults -onclose; that policy controls
only the native floating window close request (including Alt+F4)\&. Buttons accept keyboard focus and do not
start dragging\&. The global -draghandles property controls both strips, including these buttons\&. A floating
strip spans the window width and reserves space using the content labelframe's label-widget area, preserving
application pack and grid settings\&. The manager owns the content frame's label-widget options; applications
should configure children inside the content frame\&. Use -buttonside left|right (default right) at
construction or with configure to align buttons globally\&. Both docked and floating headers follow it; title
alignment is controlled independently by -titlealign\&. Changing alignment cancels an active drag without
moving content\&. It is independent of ttk button styling\&. Button appearance follows ttk; the bitmap foreground
follows the theme\&. Preview styling and hit areas continue to use -dockopts and its option-database resources\&.
.SS "LOCK BUTTON"
.PP
The lock button shows an open padlock while unlocked and a closed padlock while locked\&. Custom image
keys describe the next action: lock is displayed while unlocked, and unlock while locked\&. A dynamic
tooltip describes the action and current constraints\&. The button remains available when movement, undocking, or
deletion is blocked, so the user can release a lock\&.
Programmatic constraint changes update its image and tooltip\&. Like the other buttons it supports keyboard
activation, never starts a drag, and disappears when -draghandles is false\&.
.PP
The lock tooltip reports layout movement and undocking as locked/unlocked from the explicit -nomove
and -noundock flags, and lists the docking sides blocked by locking\&. It shows none when locking is off
or its side list is empty, even if an inactive side list is still stored\&. For example:
.PP
.RS 4n
.nf
Unlock pane
Layout movement: locked
Undocking: locked
Docking sides blocked by locking: left, top and bottom
.fi
.RE
.PP
These are current local restrictions, not predictions of the next click or a summary of every ancestor
limit\&. An unlocked movement flag does not override other constraints\&. Direct constraint updates and button
clicks refresh the tooltip in docked and floating headers\&. -tooltips false and missing Tklib disable it
exactly like the other header tooltips; no custom fallback is created\&.
.PP
-lockbuttonmode all|outer is a manager-wide property, accepted at construction and by configure, defaulting
to all\&. It controls only a button click that changes unlocked content to locked content:
.PP
.RS 4n
.nf
┌───────┬────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
│ Mode  │ Sides assigned to -lockedside when locking     │
├───────┼────────────────────────────────────────────────┤
│ all   │ {left right top bottom}                        │
│ outer │ Edges on the immediate parent split's boundary │
└───────┴────────────────────────────────────────────────┘
.fi
.RE
.PP
For a horizontal split, every child touches top and bottom; the first child also touches left and the last
also touches right\&. For a vertical split, every child touches left and right; the first also touches top and
the last also touches bottom\&. Nested leaves use their own immediate parent split, even where that split's
boundary is internal to the manager\&. Root leaves and floating content have no parent split and use all sides\&.
.PP
.RS 4n
.nf
$manager configure -draghandles true -lockbuttonmode outer
.fi
.RE
.PP
Clicking to lock sets -locked true and the computed -lockedside together, replacing any previously stored
side selection\&. Clicking again uses -locked false, clearing both movement flags while retaining the side
list\&. Sides are captured at click time: later insertion, collapse, docking, or mode changes do not recompute
them\&. To recalculate, unlock and lock again\&. Changing the mode does not change direct constraints semantics,
existing flags, or an active drag\&. The mode belongs to each manager independently, including nested managers\&.
.SS "LEAF OUTLINES"
.PP
Enable -leafborder true to surround each leaf's header and content with one independent outline\&.
.PP
The header and content share the same inner left and right edges\&. Floating windows use the same settings\&.
With leaf outlines enabled, the header draws only its bottom separator; its top and side borders are omitted\&.
-headerborderwidth sets separator thickness and -headerrelief its native bottom-edge relief\&. Flat or zero
thickness hides the separator\&. Disabling leaf outlines restores the header's complete rectangle\&.
The outline remains when -draghandles false; -showstructure continues to describe split groups separately\&.
No application widgets are recreated or reparented\&. Geometry managers reserve the border's thickness\&.
.PP
.RS 4n
.nf
$manager configure -leafborder true -leafborderwidth 2 -leafrelief ridge
$manager configure -leafopts {-color #808080}
.fi
.RE
.PP
-leafborder defaults to false, -leafborderwidth to 1, and -leafrelief to solid\&.
Relief accepts flat, solid, raised, sunken, ridge, and groove\&. Flat or zero width hides the visible line\&.
-leafopts accepts -color, the classic border's base color (default #909090); raised relief shades derive
from that color, while solid relief uses Tk's solid-border rendering\&. It does not override ttk styles\&.
Set the option database resource *splitlayoutLeafColor (class SplitlayoutLeafColor) before construction
to change the default\&. Dictionary updates are validated atomically and retain unspecified keys\&.
Changes cancel active gestures and preserve content, titles, and logical split proportions\&.
Floating borders temporarily use the content frame's background as their base color; the prior background
is restored when the border is disabled or the window is docked\&.
.SS "HEADER APPEARANCE AND TITLES"
.PP
The old three-line indicator is replaced by the header surface itself\&. The title and empty header area accept
dragging; the action buttons do not\&. Header colors and title font follow ttk theme defaults\&. Header relief and
thickness are global properties; they do not override ttk button styles:
.PP
.RS 4n
.nf
$manager configure -headerrelief ridge -headerborderwidth 2 -buttonside left
$manager title $leaf "Waveforms"
set heading [$manager title $leaf]
$manager title $leaf "" ;# Remove the heading\&.
.fi
.RE
.PP
-headerrelief accepts flat (default), raised, sunken, ridge, groove, and solid\&.
-headerborderwidth is a nonnegative integer pixel count (default 1)\&. A zero-width border is invisible\&.
Changing either property cancels an active drag and applies to existing docked and floating headers\&.
.PP
title id ?text? accepts a leaf or floating token\&. Titles belong to content, so they survive swaps, moves,
retained-content splits, docking, and undocking; removing content removes its title\&. Titles default to empty\&.
They are independent of the native floating title set by undock -title\&. Display uses one line, replacing
line breaks with spaces and shortening long headings with an ellipsis\&. The getter returns the original text\&.
Centering reserves equal space at both ends for either button alignment, keeping the title clear of buttons\&.
.SS "TITLE ALIGNMENT AND CUSTOM BUTTON IMAGES"
.PP
-titlealign left|center|right defaults to center and is independent of -buttonside\&. Left and right titles
use the respective edge of the free header area, with padding\&. Centered titles remain centered in the whole
header\&. All alignments shorten long text to avoid the buttons; the stored title remains unchanged\&.
.PP
-buttonimages is a dictionary with dock, undock, delete, lock, and unlock keys\&. Values are existing
Tk image names, not filenames or raw SVG data\&. PNG and SVG files/data can be loaded into Tk photo images first:
.PP
.RS 4n
.nf
set undockImage [image create photo -file undock\&.png]
set dockImage [image create photo -file dock\&.svg -format svg]
$manager configure -titlealign left -buttonimages [dict create undock $undockImage dock $dockImage]
$manager configure -buttonimages {delete {}} ;# Use the built-in delete bitmap\&.
.fi
.RE
.PP
Overrides apply globally to existing and future docked and floating headers\&. Partial dictionaries preserve
other entries; an empty value restores that action's built-in bitmap\&. An empty dictionary changes nothing\&.
Invalid keys or missing image names reject the whole update\&. The toggle uses undock while docked and dock
while floating\&. The lock button uses lock or unlock according to the current constraint state\&. Button widths
and header height accommodate the images at their native pixel size; images
are not scaled or recolored\&. Supply appropriately sized icons and retain them while used\&. The manager never
deletes supplied images\&. To delete one, clear/replace its override first\&. If an image is deleted externally,
a subsequent refresh falls back to the built-in bitmap\&. After changing an image's dimensions in place, reapply
its override to refresh header sizing\&. Tk photo images hold rasterized SVG output, not editable vector data\&.
.SS "OPTIONAL TOOLTIPS"
.PP
-tooltips true|false defaults to true\&. When enabled, the manager attempts package require tooltip and
uses \fITklib tooltip\fP [URL: https://core\&.tcl-lang\&.org/tklib/doc/trunk/embedded/md/tklib/files/modules/tooltip/tooltip\&.md] for the
drag area and action buttons\&. Drag-area text describes rearranging/undocking or moving/docking, depending on
whether the content is docked or floating\&. If the package cannot load, there are no tooltips and no custom
fallback\&.  Set -tooltips false to remove this manager's tooltip registrations\&. It does not disable
application tooltips globally or change their delay, font, colors, or fading\&. Setting it to true retries
loading the optional package\&.  The value returned by configure -tooltips is the requested preference, even
when the package is unavailable\&.
.SH "NESTED MANAGERS"
.PP
A leaf can contain another splitlayout instance as ordinary application content:
.PP
.RS 4n
.nf
set content [$outer frame $hostLeaf]
set inner [splitlayout $content\&.inner -draghandles true -opaqueresize false]
pack $inner -fill both -expand 1
lassign [$inner split [$inner root]] first second
.fi
.RE
.PP
Each instance owns its tree, gesture state, previews, and floating tokens\&. Starting a drag on an inner header
rearranges only the inner layout\&. Starting on the containing outer header moves the complete inner manager
with its content frame\&. An outer drop over the inner layout targets the containing outer leaf\&. An inner drop
outside the inner hull undocks that inner leaf, even if the pointer is still inside the outer manager\&.
Individual leaves cannot be transferred between managers; docking accepts only the originating manager's
floating tokens\&. An active grab prevents another manager from starting a competing content drag\&.
.PP
Undocking or redocking a containing leaf updates the actual owner toplevel of all nested managers, including
hidden instances\&. Stale gestures are cancelled, owner event bindings move to the new toplevel, and subsequent
deferred sash previews use that window as their transient owner\&. Independently floating descendants keep
their own toplevel ownership\&. Mapping and gesture entry also refresh ownership after external Tk promotion\&.
Removing the containing content destroys its nested managers and their owned floating windows\&.
.PP
Minimum sizes are independent between instances\&. The outer manager does not infer constraints from embedded
application widgets\&. To constrain the host leaf, set its content minimum from the inner root's requiredsize,
adding any hull padding or other surrounding widgets, and refresh it when the inner requirements change\&.
.SH "SWAPPING AND REMOVING CONTENT"
.PP
The following operations continue the example above:
.PP
.RS 4n
.nf
# Exchange complete content frames across different nesting levels\&.
\&.layout swap $left $bottom

# Remove the middle pane and all widgets it contains\&.
set survivingParent [\&.layout remove $middle]

# Keep top, destroy its sibling subtree, and replace their parent with top\&.
\&.layout retain $top
.fi
.RE
.PP
swap exchanges content frames; node identifiers continue to identify the same logical positions\&. Cached frame
and application-widget pathnames remain valid, but may now belong to a different leaf\&.
.PP
remove destroys the requested subtree\&. If its parent has only one child left, that child replaces the parent\&.
retain destroys all siblings of the retained node and collapses its immediate parent; it does not collapse all
ancestors\&. Retaining the root does nothing\&.
.PP
To discard all content and start again:
.PP
.RS 4n
.nf
set root [\&.layout clear]
.fi
.RE
.PP
Previously removed node identifiers are invalid\&. Use the return values or query the layout again after
structural changes\&. To destroy the entire layout, use \&.layout destroy or destroy \&.layout\&.
.SH "VISUAL STRUCTURE"
.PP
Enable outlines for the whole layout to distinguish flat sibling arrangements from nested groups:
.PP
.RS 4n
.nf
\&.layout configure -showstructure true
# Also accepted at construction:
splitlayout \&.outlined -draghandles true -showstructure true
.fi
.RE
.PP
By default, each split receives a one-pixel gray outline and two pixels of inner spacing, giving a three-pixel
inset on each side\&.  Customize these dimensions and colors with -structureopts or the option database
described below\&.  Flat siblings share one enclosing outline; a nested split has an additional inset outline
around its children\&. A sole root leaf has no split outline\&. The setting is independent of drag handles and
applies to future splits\&. During docking, the receiving group outline turns blue alongside the existing
destination preview\&. For a local edge move that creates a new split, the highlighted outline is the current
enclosing group\&. Escape, an invalid destination, and release restore the normal outline color\&.
.PP
Changing -showstructure cancels active gestures and rebuilds geometry while retaining content, native
panedwindows, and split proportions (subject to available pixel space)\&. Disabling it removes the outlines and
their spacing\&.  Very small panes can still collapse under Tk's normal geometry constraints\&.
.PP
With this option enabled, widget split returns the enclosing outline frame\&. Use panedwindow split to obtain
the native ttk panedwindow regardless of the option value, for example:
.PP
.RS 4n
.nf
set split [\&.layout root]
set pw [\&.layout panedwindow $split]
set position [$pw sashpos 0]
.fi
.RE
.PP
node recognizes both paths\&. container split returns the outer geometry container, while container $pw
returns the outline frame that packs the native panedwindow\&. tree includes both widget and panedwindow
for splits; widgets exposes the actual geometry containers\&. Outline frames remain immediate Tk children of
the hull\&.
.SH RESIZING
.PP
Enable deferred resizing at construction or through configure:
.PP
.RS 4n
.nf
\&.layout configure -opaqueresize false -sashpreview window
.fi
.RE
.PP
In deferred mode, dragging changes only the preview target\&. Releasing the mouse commits the sash position and
resizes the content\&. Escape cancels the gesture\&. Changing layout structure or resizing mode, unmapping the
pane, or changing geometry in a way that invalidates the gesture also cancels it\&.
.PP
.RS 4n
.nf
┌──────────────┬─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
│ Preview mode │ Behavior                                                            │
├──────────────┼─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤
│ window       │ Uses a temporary borderless transient toplevel for the sash marker\&. │
│ inline       │ Uses a frame inside the layout for the sash marker\&.                 │
│ none         │ Defers resizing without displaying a marker\&.                        │
└──────────────┴─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘
.fi
.RE
.PP
Preview motion is coalesced at idle\&. window can reduce content exposures on composited desktops, but
appearance depends on Tk and the window manager; it does not guarantee atomic repainting of application
widgets\&.
.SH OPTIONS
.PP
.RS 4n
.nf
┌────────────────────┬─────────────────┬────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
│ Option             │ Default         │ Meaning                                                                    │
├────────────────────┼─────────────────┼────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤
│ -width             │ 800             │ Nonnegative requested hull width in pixels\&.                                │
│ -height            │ 600             │ Nonnegative requested hull height in pixels\&.                               │
│ -opaqueresize      │ true            │ Resize pane content continuously while dragging a sash\&.                    │
│ -sashpreview       │ window          │ Deferred preview mode: window, inline, or none\&.                            │
│ -lockbuttonmode    │ all             │ Lock-button side policy: all edges or immediate parent split boundaries\&.   │
│ -buttonside        │ right           │ Place header buttons on the left or right; title alignment is independent\&. │
│ -titlealign        │ center          │ Align the optional header title left, center, or right\&.                    │
│ -buttonimages      │ Empty values    │ Optional dock, undock, delete, lock, and unlock Tk image overrides\&.        │
│ -headerrelief      │ flat            │ Rectangular header relief\&.                                                 │
│ -leafborder        │ false           │ Outline each leaf, including floating windows\&.                             │
│ -leafborderwidth   │ 1               │ Nonnegative leaf outline thickness in pixels\&.                              │
│ -leafrelief        │ solid           │ Leaf outline relief, independent of the header\&.                            │
│ -leafopts          │ option database │ Leaf border base color (-color)\&.                                           │
│ -headerborderwidth │ 1               │ Nonnegative header border thickness in pixels\&.                             │
│ -tooltips          │ true            │ Use Tklib tooltips when available\&.                                         │
│ -draghandles       │ false           │ Show docking grips on all current and future leaves\&.                       │
│ -showstructure     │ false           │ Outline and inset all current and future splits\&.                           │
└────────────────────┴─────────────────┴────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘
.fi
.RE
.PP
These options apply to current and future splits\&. Deferred mode affects sash dragging; resizing the containing
window and programmatic sizing still update the layout normally\&. Very small containers can collapse panes;
there is no per-pane minimum-size API\&.
.PP
The class uses oo::configurable properties directly, without creating option objects\&. Query a property with
configure -name\&. With no arguments, configure returns a dictionary of property names and current values,
rather than Tk-style option descriptors:
.PP
.RS 4n
.nf
set preview [\&.layout configure -sashpreview]
set properties [\&.layout configure]
\&.layout configure -opaqueresize false -padding 4
.fi
.RE
.PP
The hull properties -width, -height, -padding, -borderwidth, -relief, -cursor, -takefocus, and
-style delegate to the ttk frame\&. -class is read-only\&. The constructor accepts the six creation options
listed above and the three appearance dictionaries described below; other writable properties can be set after
construction\&. No Tk option-database resources are added for -opaqueresize, -sashpreview, -draghandles, or
-showstructure\&.
.PP
Native property setters run in argument order\&. If a later value is invalid, earlier successful changes remain
in effect\&. Invalid resizing values leave that property's value and any active drag unchanged; changing a valid
resizing value cancels the active drag\&. Setting the same value preserves it\&.
.SH "APPEARANCE AND DRAG DETECTION"
.PP
Manager-owned decoration and drag detection settings follow graphtoolbar's Tk option-database convention:
package-specific resource names, defaults registered at widgetDefault priority, and dictionary properties for
explicit per-instance overrides\&. No option instances are created\&.
.PP
.RS 4n
.nf
package require splitlayout

option add *splitlayoutDockColor orange userDefault
option add *splitlayoutDockOuterWidth 20 userDefault
option add *splitlayoutDockEdgeWidth 32 userDefault
option add *splitlayoutStructureColor gray55 userDefault
option add *splitlayoutStructureActiveColor orange userDefault

splitlayout::splitlayout \&.layout -draghandles true -showstructure true -dockopts {-threshold 8} -structureopts {-padding 3}
pack \&.layout -fill both -expand 1

# Partial updates keep all unspecified settings\&.
\&.layout configure -dockopts {-color purple -outerwidth 24}
\&.layout configure -sashpreviewopts {-color gray40 -width 5}
set docking [\&.layout configure -dockopts]
.fi
.RE
.PP
.RS 4n
.nf
┌──────────────────┬──────────────┬─────────────────────────────────┬─────────────────────────────────┬─────────┐
│ Property         │ Key          │ Resource name                   │ Resource class                  │ Default │
├──────────────────┼──────────────┼─────────────────────────────────┼─────────────────────────────────┼─────────┤
│ -dockopts        │ -color       │ splitlayoutDockColor            │ SplitlayoutDockColor            │ #448aff │
│ -dockopts        │ -linewidth   │ splitlayoutDockLineWidth        │ SplitlayoutDockLineWidth        │ 3       │
│ -dockopts        │ -edgewidth   │ splitlayoutDockEdgeWidth        │ SplitlayoutDockEdgeWidth        │ 24      │
│ -dockopts        │ -outerwidth  │ splitlayoutDockOuterWidth       │ SplitlayoutDockOuterWidth       │ 12      │
│ -dockopts        │ -threshold   │ splitlayoutDockThreshold        │ SplitlayoutDockThreshold        │ 5       │
│ -leafopts        │ -color       │ splitlayoutLeafColor            │ SplitlayoutLeafColor            │ #909090 │
│ -structureopts   │ -color       │ splitlayoutStructureColor       │ SplitlayoutStructureColor       │ #909090 │
│ -structureopts   │ -activecolor │ splitlayoutStructureActiveColor │ SplitlayoutStructureActiveColor │ #448aff │
│ -structureopts   │ -borderwidth │ splitlayoutStructureBorderWidth │ SplitlayoutStructureBorderWidth │ 1       │
│ -structureopts   │ -padding     │ splitlayoutStructurePadding     │ SplitlayoutStructurePadding     │ 2       │
│ -sashpreviewopts │ -color       │ splitlayoutSashPreviewColor     │ SplitlayoutSashPreviewColor     │ #606060 │
│ -sashpreviewopts │ -width       │ splitlayoutSashPreviewWidth     │ SplitlayoutSashPreviewWidth     │ 3       │
└──────────────────┴──────────────┴─────────────────────────────────┴─────────────────────────────────┴─────────┘
.fi
.RE
.PP
The database is queried against the layout hull once during construction\&. Standard Tk name/class patterns and
priorities apply; for example, *editor\&.layout\&.splitlayoutDockColor scopes a resource to a layout below
\&.editor\&.  Explicit constructor dictionary keys override database values\&. Later database changes affect newly
created layouts only; use configure for an existing layout\&. Built-in defaults are still available if the
application calls option clear\&.  The three dictionaries are accepted both at construction and by configure;
an empty dictionary leaves current settings unchanged\&.
.PP
All dimensions are integer \fBscreen pixels\fP, from zero to 2147483647, without additional DPI scaling\&. Colors
must be valid Tk colors\&. Preview line widths (-dockopts -linewidth and -sashpreviewopts -width) must be at
least one\&.
.TP
\(bu
-dockopts -color colors all docking preview rectangles\&. -linewidth controls the center-swap outline and sash insertion line; edge-preview bands use the corresponding detection width\&. Preview strokes are clipped to the region\&.
.TP
\(bu
-outerwidth specifies the band inside the hull for docking beside the entire layout, capped at one eighth of each hull dimension\&. Zero disables outer docking zones\&.
.TP
\(bu
-edgewidth specifies each leaf's local edge band, capped at one quarter of each leaf dimension\&. Zero disables local edge moves, allowing center swaps there instead\&. Native sash and outer-zone precedence remain unchanged\&.
.TP
\(bu
-threshold specifies the minimum movement along either screen axis before a grip drag activates\&. Zero activates on the first motion event; pressing and releasing without motion still does nothing\&.
.TP
\(bu
-structureopts controls the normal and receiving-group outline colors, border thickness, and inner spacing\&. The inset per side is -borderwidth + -padding\&. A zero border width hides the outline; padding can also be zero\&.
.TP
\(bu
-sashpreviewopts controls the manager's deferred-resize marker in both window and inline modes; none remains invisible\&.
.PP
Dictionary updates validate all supplied values before changing anything\&. An invalid key, color, or distance
leaves that dictionary and any active gesture intact\&. Successful changes cancel active gestures; unchanged
assignments do nothing\&. Structure changes rebuild geometry while retaining content and proportions\&. Each
dictionary update is atomic; separate property/value pairs retain the usual ordered oo::configurable
behavior\&.
.PP
These settings do not alter ttk styles, grip colors, or native sash appearance and hit areas\&. The existing
behavior properties (-draghandles, -showstructure, -opaqueresize, and -sashpreview) remain separate
from database styling\&.
.SH "FLOATING MANAGED CONTENT"
.PP
undock promotes an existing leaf's content frame to a native window, removes its leaf from the docked tree,
and collapses a parent with only one remaining child\&. The application widgets, paths, bindings, and content
state are retained\&. dock returns that owned content to a new leaf\&. Arbitrary windows and other managers'
floating content are not accepted\&. For mouse-driven docking and undocking, see Dragging panes\&.
.PP
.RS 4n
.nf
set content [$manager frame $leaf]
set floating [$manager undock $leaf -title "Waveform" -geometry 600x400+100+100]

# The content frame is now itself the native toplevel\&.
wm title $content "Floating waveform"
set info [$manager floating $floating]
set allFloating [$manager floating]

# Return it beside an existing leaf or split; the original leaf ID has expired\&.
set newLeaf [$manager dock $floating -right $target]
.fi
.RE
.PP
The docking destinations are -left, -right, -top, -bottom, -before, and -after, with the same
placement rules as move\&. Specify at most one\&. Edge destinations accept leaves or splits; sibling destinations
require a non-root target\&. Docking reuses a matching parent orientation, converts a leaf target into a split
when necessary, or wraps a split target in a new parent\&. Existing pane shares are divided equally at the
insertion point\&.
.PP
With no destination, dock tries the sibling positions saved by undock, nearest first, with the original
orientation and relative order (following siblings take precedence at equal distance)\&. The first surviving
sibling node is used, including if it has become a split\&. Node identity represents a position: swapping its
content does not invalidate it\&. If it has moved, docking follows that node at its current location\&. Deleted
anchors are skipped\&. Original sash proportions are not restored\&. Explicit destinations override this
restoration\&. If no anchor remains, dock places the content to the right of the current root\&. Undocking the
sole root creates an empty placeholder leaf to preserve the nonempty-tree invariant\&. An implicit dock replaces
that placeholder if it is still the root, has no header title, and its content frame has no child widgets\&. If
you populate it, both contents are retained\&. Explicit destinations always follow the requested placement
instead of replacing the placeholder\&.
.PP
undock accepts -title (default splitlayout), -geometry (default current content size and position), and
-onclose dock|destroy (default dock)\&. The native close button therefore returns content to the current
layout by default, trying its remembered position before using the fallback destination\&. Use -onclose destroy
to discard the content on close, or $manager remove $floating to destroy it explicitly\&. $manager clear and
removing the docked root clear only the docked tree; floating windows remain owned by the manager\&. Destroying
the manager or an actual Tk ancestor destroys all owned floating windows as well\&. Direct destruction of a
floating frame removes its record\&.
.PP
Floating tokens are opaque, manager-specific handles\&. They expire on docking or removal, and must not be
reused\&.  floating lists these tokens; floating $token returns a dictionary with id, frame, title,
geometry, state, and onclose\&. frame $token returns the retained frame\&. Docked-tree commands (tree,
leaves, node, and locate) exclude floating content; widgets still exposes its actual Tk ancestry and
wm geometry manager\&.
.PP
Content frames are \fBclassic labelframe widgets with class Frame\fP, borderless while docked, because Tk's
wm manage cannot promote ttk::frame\&. The hull, panedwindows, and header buttons retain their ttk behavior;
leaf wrappers use classic frames for configurable borders\&. Code using frame $leaf for pack, grid, or
child creation continues to work; ttk-only frame
options such as -style are no longer available on that content frame\&. Place themed widgets inside it when
needed\&. Actual parentage remains the layout hull throughout the round trip\&.
.PP
Tk default bindtags follow the changed toplevel\&. The manager also retargets explicit bindtag entries naming the
old toplevel, restoring unchanged custom lists on docking and preserving other edits made while
floating\&. Binding scripts containing literal window paths are not rewritten\&. Applications that cache winfo
toplevel should query it again after a docking transition\&. Preserve manager-owned bindtags if changing a
floating frame's bindtags directly\&.
.PP
Window promotion uses Tk's wm manage/wm forget, so native activation, positioning, and decorations depend
on the platform/window manager\&. Invalid destinations are rejected before unmanaging the floating
window\&. Failure to promote or apply the requested geometry restores the docked layout\&. Windows and Aqua
behavior should be verified on their native Tk builds; X11 test results alone do not establish macOS
compatibility\&.
.SH "INSPECTING THE LAYOUT"
.PP
Use logical node identifiers when working with the layout structure, and widget pathnames when working with Tk:
.PP
.RS 4n
.nf
set leaf [lindex [\&.layout leaves] 0]
set contentFrame [\&.layout frame $leaf]

set logicalParent [\&.layout parent $leaf]
set geometryContainer [\&.layout container $leaf]
set actualParent [winfo parent $contentFrame]

set layoutTree [\&.layout tree]
set widgetTree [\&.layout widgets]
.fi
.RE
.PP
For a non-root node identifier, container returns its parent panedwindow\&. Its actual Tk parent remains
\&.layout\&.  With handles enabled, passing the content frame pathname instead returns its pane wrapper\&. winfo
manager reports the manager name, not the pathname of that geometry container\&.
.PP
node maps an exact managed frame or panedwindow pathname to its node identifier\&. locate accepts a descendant
application widget and walks its Tk parents to find the containing leaf:
.PP
.RS 4n
.nf
set leaf [\&.layout node $contentFrame]
# For any existing application widget inside a content frame:
set applicationWidget [lindex [winfo children $contentFrame] 0]
if {$applicationWidget ne {}} {
    set leaf [\&.layout locate $applicationWidget]
}
.fi
.RE
.PP
tree returns nested dictionaries containing id, type, parent, widget, and children\&. Leaves also
contain frame\&. Splits contain panedwindow, orient, and proportions, plus ratio when they have two
children\&. Split children are nested node dictionaries in pane order\&.
.PP
widgets returns nested dictionaries containing path, parent, class, manager, container, and
children\&.  It includes application widgets\&. The container is resolved for layout nodes and for widgets
managed by pack, grid, or place; it is empty for other managers without a supported reverse lookup\&.
.PP
Floating content is described separately by floating; widgets still includes its actual Tk subtree\&.
.PP
Both snapshots are for inspection\&. They do not serialize application widget state or provide a restore
operation\&.
.SH CLASSES
.PP
.RS -4n
\fBsplitlayout\fR (::splitlayout)
.RE
.PP
Configurable TclOO layout manager for Tcl/Tk 9\&.
.PP
configure is inherited from oo::configurable\&. With no arguments it returns a dictionary of property
names and values; with one property name it returns that value\&. Set properties with option/value
pairs\&. Pairs are applied in order, and a failed setter leaves earlier successful assignments in
effect\&. Use configure -name instead of cget -name\&.
.PP
Hull properties delegate directly to the ttk frame\&. Layout behavior properties use oo::configurable
directly, without option objects\&. Appearance dictionaries take their initial values from the Tk option
database; see the namespace resource reference\&.
.PP
\fBMethod summary\fR
.RS 4n
.TP
constructor
Constructor for the class\&.
.TP
destructor
Destructor for the class\&.
.TP
\fIconfigure\fP [URL: https://www\&.tcl-lang\&.org/man/tcl9\&.0/TclCmd/index\&.html]
Configure properties\&. See \fI::oo::configuresupport::configurable\fP [URL: https://www\&.tcl-lang\&.org/man/tcl9\&.0/TclCmd/index\&.html]\&.
.TP
children
Returns the immediate logical children of a node\&. Child identifiers in pane order, or an empty list for a leaf\&.
.TP
clear
Destroys the docked tree and creates one empty root leaf; floating content is retained\&.
.TP
constraints
Queries or atomically updates restrictions for one node or floating content\&.
.TP
container
Returns the geometry container for a managed node\&. The hull, parent panedwindow, or enclosing leaf/split wrapper as appropriate\&.
.TP
dock
Returns owned floating content to a new leaf in the docked layout\&. The new docked leaf identifier\&.
.TP
floating
Lists floating content or describes one floating window owned by this manager\&.
.TP
frame
Returns the retained content frame of a docked leaf or floating window\&. The content frame pathname, unchanged across docking transitions\&.
.TP
insert
Inserts an empty leaf beside an existing non-root node\&.
.TP
leaves
Collects the leaves below a logical node\&.
.TP
locate
Finds the leaf containing an application widget\&.
.TP
minsize
Gets or sets the minimum content-area dimensions of a leaf or floating window\&.
.TP
move
Moves an existing leaf to an edge or sibling position without destroying its content\&.
.TP
node
Finds the logical node associated with an exact widget pathname\&.
.TP
panedwindow
Returns the native panedwindow of a split, independently of structure visibility\&. The native ttk panedwindow pathname\&. Leaf identifiers raise an error\&.
.TP
parent
Returns the logical parent of a node\&. The parent node identifier, or an empty string for the root\&.
.TP
proportions
Queries or sets all pane proportions of a split\&.
.TP
ratio
Queries or sets the first pane fraction of a two-child split\&.
.TP
remove
Removes a docked subtree or destroys owned floating content\&.
.TP
requiredsize
Calculates a node's minimum outer size, including manager-owned decoration\&.
.TP
retain
Keeps a node while removing its siblings and collapsing its parent\&.
.TP
root
Returns the current logical root node\&. The root node identifier\&.
.TP
\fIsplit\fP [URL: https://www\&.tcl-lang\&.org/man/tcl9\&.0/TclCmd/index\&.html]
Replaces a leaf with a split containing two new leaves\&.
.TP
swap
Exchanges the content frames assigned to two leaves\&.
.TP
title
Gets or sets the optional header title belonging to managed content\&.
.TP
tree
Returns a recursive snapshot of the logical layout hierarchy\&. A nested dictionary describing the requested logical subtree\&.
.TP
type
Returns the type of a logical node\&. The string leaf or split\&.
.TP
undock
Removes a leaf from the layout and promotes its retained content to a floating window\&.
.TP
widget
Returns the widget associated with a logical node\&. The outer geometry widget: a leaf wrapper with drag handles, a split wrapper with showstructure, or the content frame/native panedwindow otherwise\&. Use panedwindow to access a split's native widget\&.
.TP
widgets
Returns a recursive snapshot of the actual Tk widget hierarchy\&. A nested widget dictionary with path, parent, class, manager, container, and children\&.
.RE
.PP
\fBProperties\fR
.RS 4n
.TP
-borderwidth
Readable, writable\&. Hull border width, delegated to the ttk frame\&.
.TP
-buttonimages
Readable, writable\&. Dictionary mapping dock, undock, delete, lock, and unlock to existing Tk image names\&. Empty values select built-in bitmaps and are the default\&. Updates merge atomically; an empty value restores one default\&. Supplied images are application-owned\&. Buttons and header height accommodate image dimensions\&. Changing images cancels an active drag\&. Empty dictionaries leave existing overrides unchanged\&.
.TP
-buttonside
Readable, writable\&. Side of the grip strip containing the action buttons: left or right; default right\&. Applies immediately to docked and floating headers\&. Title alignment is controlled independently by titlealign\&. Changing the value cancels an active content drag\&. Button appearance remains controlled by ttk\&.
.TP
-class
Readable\&. Read-only hull class override; empty when the default ttk class is used\&.
.TP
-cursor
Readable, writable\&. Hull cursor, delegated to the ttk frame\&.
.TP
-dockopts
Readable, writable\&. Dictionary of docking preview and detection settings: -color, -linewidth, -edgewidth, -outerwidth, and -threshold\&. Defaults come from the Tk option database\&. Partial updates retain other keys\&.
.TP
-draghandles
Readable, writable\&. Boolean enabling manager-owned rectangular headers for every leaf; default false\&. Drag a grip to swap at a leaf center, move at an edge, or insert at a sash\&. Escape cancels; an outside drop undocks\&. Floating grips move and dock owned content\&. Disabling removes docked and floating grips without destroying content\&. This setting is independent of -opaqueresize and applies to future leaves as well\&.
.TP
-headerborderwidth
Readable, writable\&. Nonnegative integer header border thickness in pixels; default 1\&. Changes cancel an active drag and update the reserved header height\&.
.TP
-headerrelief
Readable, writable\&. Header relief: flat, raised, sunken, ridge, groove, or solid; default flat\&. Controls the full header rectangle, or only its bottom separator when leafborder is enabled\&. Button styling remains controlled by ttk\&. Changes cancel an active drag\&.
.TP
-height
Readable, writable\&. Requested hull height, delegated to the ttk frame; constructor default 600\&.
.TP
-leafborder
Readable, writable\&. Boolean enabling a border around each leaf's header and content; default false\&. Works for floating windows and with draghandles disabled, independently of showstructure\&.
.TP
-leafborderwidth
Readable, writable\&. Nonnegative integer leaf border thickness in pixels; default 1\&. Zero hides the border\&.
.TP
-leafopts
Readable, writable\&. Dictionary with -color, the classic leaf border base color; default #909090\&. Reads splitlayoutLeafColor / SplitlayoutLeafColor from the option database\&. Changes cancel gestures, preserve content, and update docked and floating leaves\&.
.TP
-leafrelief
Readable, writable\&. Leaf border relief: flat, solid, raised, sunken, ridge, or groove; default solid\&.
.TP
-lockbuttonmode
Readable, writable\&. Lock-button policy: all or outer; default all\&. All blocks all four edges\&. Outer computes edges on the immediate parent split boundary when clicked\&. Root leaves and floating content use all four edges\&. Changing the mode does not rewrite existing constraints or affect direct commands\&.
.TP
-opaqueresize
Readable, writable\&. Boolean enabling live sash resizing; default true\&. Values are normalized to 0 or 1\&. Changing the value cancels an active deferred drag without committing its target\&.
.TP
-padding
Readable, writable\&. Internal hull padding, delegated to the ttk frame\&.
.TP
-relief
Readable, writable\&. Hull relief, delegated to the ttk frame\&.
.TP
-sashpreview
Readable, writable\&. Deferred sash preview: window, inline, or none; default window\&. Changing the value cancels an active deferred drag\&. Invalid values preserve the old value\&.
.TP
-sashpreviewopts
Readable, writable\&. Dictionary of deferred sash preview settings: -color and -width\&. Applies to window and inline preview modes\&. Defaults come from the Tk option database\&. Partial updates retain other keys\&.
.TP
-showstructure
Readable, writable\&. Boolean showing an outline and inset around every split; default false\&. Appearance is set by -structureopts\&. Applies to existing and future splits\&. Changing it cancels active gestures, preserves content, and rebuilds geometry\&. During docking the receiving split outline is highlighted\&.
.TP
-structureopts
Readable, writable\&. Dictionary of split outline settings: -color, -activecolor, -borderwidth, and -padding\&. Defaults come from the Tk option database\&. Partial updates retain other keys\&.
.TP
-style
Readable, writable\&. Hull ttk style, delegated to the ttk frame\&.
.TP
-takefocus
Readable, writable\&. Hull focus traversal setting, delegated to the ttk frame\&.
.TP
-titlealign
Readable, writable\&. Header title alignment: left, center, or right; default center\&. Independent of buttonside\&. Applies to docked and floating headers\&. Titles are truncated within the space clear of buttons\&.
.TP
-tooltips
Readable, writable\&. Boolean enabling drag-area and header-button tooltips when the optional Tklib tooltip package loads; default true\&. Missing packages are silently tolerated\&. This property expresses the preference, not package availability\&. Only this manager's registrations are changed; Tklib's global settings remain application-controlled\&.
.TP
-width
Readable, writable\&. Requested hull width, delegated to the ttk frame; constructor default 800\&.
.RE
.PP
.RS -4n
\fBconstructor\fR (::splitlayout::splitlayout)
.RE
.PP
Creates a split layout with one empty root leaf\&.
.RS 4n
\fBsplitlayout create OBJNAME\fP \fIpath ?-width width? ?-height height? ?-opaqueresize boolean? ?-sashpreview mode? ?-draghandles boolean? ?-buttonside side? ?-showstructure boolean? ?-dockopts dictionary? ?-structureopts dictionary? ?-sashpreviewopts dictionary? ?-leafborder boolean? ?-leafborderwidth pixels? ?-leafrelief relief? ?-leafopts dictionary? ?-headerrelief relief? ?-headerborderwidth pixels? ?-tooltips boolean? ?-titlealign alignment? ?-buttonimages dictionary? ?-lockbuttonmode mode?\fP
.br
\fBsplitlayout new\fP \fIpath ?-width width? ?-height height? ?-opaqueresize boolean? ?-sashpreview mode? ?-draghandles boolean? ?-buttonside side? ?-showstructure boolean? ?-dockopts dictionary? ?-structureopts dictionary? ?-sashpreviewopts dictionary? ?-leafborder boolean? ?-leafborderwidth pixels? ?-leafrelief relief? ?-leafopts dictionary? ?-headerrelief relief? ?-headerborderwidth pixels? ?-tooltips boolean? ?-titlealign alignment? ?-buttonimages dictionary? ?-lockbuttonmode mode?\fP
.br
.RE
.PP
\fBParameters\fR
.RS 4n
.TP
-buttonimages dictionary
Optional dock, undock, delete, lock, and unlock images; default built-in bitmaps\&.
.TP
-buttonside side
Align header buttons left or right in docked and floating content; default right\&.
.TP
-dockopts dictionary
Docking colors, preview line width, and detection distances; option-database defaults\&.
.TP
-draghandles boolean
Enable rectangular docking headers on all leaves; default false\&.
.TP
-headerborderwidth pixels
Nonnegative header border thickness; default 1\&.
.TP
-headerrelief relief
Header border: flat, raised, sunken, ridge, groove, or solid; default flat\&.
.TP
-height height
Nonnegative requested hull height in pixels; default 600\&.
.TP
-leafborder boolean
Enable independent leaf outlines; default false\&.
.TP
-leafborderwidth pixels
Nonnegative leaf outline thickness; default 1\&.
.TP
-leafopts dictionary
Leaf border base color; option-database defaults\&.
.TP
-leafrelief relief
Leaf border relief; default solid\&.
.TP
-lockbuttonmode mode
Lock-button side policy all or outer; default all\&.
.TP
-opaqueresize boolean
Enable live sash resizing; default true\&.
.TP
-sashpreview mode
Deferred preview mode: window, inline, or none; default window\&.
.TP
-sashpreviewopts dictionary
Deferred sash preview color and width; option-database defaults\&.
.TP
-showstructure boolean
Outline and inset all split regions; default false\&.
.TP
-structureopts dictionary
Split outline colors, border width, and spacing; option-database defaults\&.
.TP
-titlealign alignment
Header title alignment: left, center, or right; default center\&.
.TP
-tooltips boolean
Show Tklib tooltips when the optional package is available; default true\&.
.TP
-width width
Nonnegative requested hull width in pixels; default 800\&.
.TP
path
New, non-root Tk widget pathname\&.
.RE
.PP
\fBDescription\fR
.PP
The hull is a ttk frame\&. Managed content frames and panedwindows are its immediate Tk children; their logical
layout hierarchy is stored separately\&. The object command takes the widget pathname\&. Existing widgets or
commands at that pathname, invalid sizes, and invalid options raise an error\&.
.PP
\fBReturn value\fR
.PP
Nothing\&.
.PP
.RS -4n
\fBdestructor\fR (::splitlayout::splitlayout)
.RE
.PP
Destroys the layout and all of its content widgets\&.
.RS 4n
\fBSPLITLAYOUTOBJ destroy\fP
.br
.RE
.PP
\fBDescription\fR
.PP
Cancels deferred dragging and pending geometry callbacks, removes the manager binding scripts and owner
bindtag, and destroys the owned hull\&. Also supports cleanup after partially completed construction\&.
.PP
\fBReturn value\fR
.PP
Nothing\&.
.PP
.RS -4n
\fBchildren\fR (::splitlayout::splitlayout)
.RE
.PP
Returns the immediate logical children of a node\&.
Child identifiers in pane order, or an empty list for a leaf\&.
.RS 4n
\fBSPLITLAYOUTOBJ children\fP \fIid\fP
.br
.RE
.PP
\fBParameters\fR
.RS 4n
.TP
id
Existing logical node identifier\&.
.RE
.PP
\fBReturn value\fR
.PP
Returns the immediate logical children of a node\&.
Child identifiers in pane order, or an empty list for a leaf\&.
.PP
.RS -4n
\fBclear\fR (::splitlayout::splitlayout)
.RE
.PP
Destroys the docked tree and creates one empty root leaf; floating content is retained\&.
.RS 4n
\fBSPLITLAYOUTOBJ clear\fP
.br
.RE
.PP
\fBReturn value\fR
.PP
The new root leaf identifier\&.
.PP
.RS -4n
\fBconstraints\fR (::splitlayout::splitlayout)
.RE
.PP
Queries or atomically updates restrictions for one node or floating content\&.
.RS 4n
\fBSPLITLAYOUTOBJ constraints\fP \fIid ?option? | id ?-nomove boolean? ?-noundock boolean? ?-nodestroy boolean? ?-noresize boolean? ?-locked boolean? ?-lockedside sides? ?-minleaves count? ?-maxleaves count? ?-maxdepth depth?\fP
.br
.RE
.PP
\fBParameters\fR
.RS 4n
.TP
id
Leaf, split, or owned floating token\&.
.TP
args
No arguments, one option name, or option/value pairs\&.
.RE
.PP
\fBDescription\fR
.PP
Leaf flags follow content; split flags and subtree limits belong to node identity\&. Movement flags and
locked are leaf/floating-only\&. Floating content has no assignable subtree limits\&. Queries show explicit
settings, not restrictions inherited by an operation from protected descendants\&. Locked also protects
adjacent move/dock destinations on lockedside edges, in addition to setting the two movement flags\&.
Lockedside is a list of left/right/top/bottom; defaults to all four, and empty disables boundary protection\&.
.PP
\fBReturn value\fR
.PP
One queried value, or the complete explicit settings dictionary\&.
.PP
.RS -4n
\fBcontainer\fR (::splitlayout::splitlayout)
.RE
.PP
Returns the geometry container for a managed node\&.
The hull, parent panedwindow, or enclosing leaf/split wrapper as appropriate\&.
.RS 4n
\fBSPLITLAYOUTOBJ container\fP \fIvalue\fP
.br
.RE
.PP
\fBParameters\fR
.RS 4n
.TP
value
Logical node identifier or exact managed widget pathname\&.
.RE
.PP
\fBDescription\fR
.PP
The container can differ from the actual Tk parent\&. Unknown identifiers and unmanaged paths raise an error\&.
.PP
A node identifier or wrapper path resolves to the outer geometry container\&. A content frame pathname
resolves to its pane wrapper when handles are enabled\&. A native panedwindow path resolves to its
enclosing split wrapper when showstructure is enabled\&.
.PP
\fBReturn value\fR
.PP
Returns the geometry container for a managed node\&.
The hull, parent panedwindow, or enclosing leaf/split wrapper as appropriate\&.
.PP
.RS -4n
\fBdock\fR (::splitlayout::splitlayout)
.RE
.PP
Returns owned floating content to a new leaf in the docked layout\&.
The new docked leaf identifier\&.
.RS 4n
\fBSPLITLAYOUTOBJ dock\fP \fIfloating ?-left target|-right target|-top target|-bottom target|-before target|-after target?\fP
.br
.RE
.PP
\fBParameters\fR
.RS 4n
.TP
-after target
Dock after a non-root node in its existing parent\&.
.TP
-before target
Dock before a non-root node in its existing parent\&.
.TP
-bottom target
Dock below a leaf or split\&.
.TP
-left target
Dock to the left of a leaf or split\&.
.TP
-right target
Dock to the right of a leaf or split\&.
.TP
-top target
Dock above a leaf or split\&.
.TP
floating
Token returned by this manager's undock command\&.
.RE
.PP
\fBDescription\fR
.PP
At most one destination is allowed\&. With no destination, tries the saved sibling positions in the original
orientation, then replaces an untouched root placeholder or docks to the right of the current root\&. Explicit
destinations follow move semantics: reuse a matching parent, convert a leaf target to a split, or wrap a split
target in a new split\&.
The floating token expires; all content widgets retain their pathnames and state\&. Arbitrary windows and
other managers' tokens are rejected\&. Custom toplevel bindtags are adjusted; literal paths in scripts are not\&.
.PP
\fBReturn value\fR
.PP
Returns owned floating content to a new leaf in the docked layout\&.
The new docked leaf identifier\&.
.PP
.RS -4n
\fBfloating\fR (::splitlayout::splitlayout)
.RE
.PP
Lists floating content or describes one floating window owned by this manager\&.
.RS 4n
\fBSPLITLAYOUTOBJ floating\fP \fI?id?\fP
.br
.RE
.PP
\fBParameters\fR
.RS 4n
.TP
id
Floating token returned by undock; omit to list tokens\&. Optional, default ""\&.
.RE
.PP
\fBDescription\fR
.PP
Floating content is absent from tree, leaves, node, and locate\&. Its actual widgets remain visible in widgets\&.
The snapshot contains id, frame, title, geometry, state, and onclose\&. Tokens expire on docking or removal\&.
.PP
\fBReturn value\fR
.PP
A list of floating tokens, or a dictionary describing one floating window\&.
.PP
.RS -4n
\fBframe\fR (::splitlayout::splitlayout)
.RE
.PP
Returns the retained content frame of a docked leaf or floating window\&.
The content frame pathname, unchanged across docking transitions\&.
.RS 4n
\fBSPLITLAYOUTOBJ frame\fP \fIid\fP
.br
.RE
.PP
\fBParameters\fR
.RS 4n
.TP
id
Existing leaf identifier or floating token\&.
.RE
.PP
\fBDescription\fR
.PP
Raises an error if the node is unknown or is a split\&.
.PP
\fBReturn value\fR
.PP
Returns the retained content frame of a docked leaf or floating window\&.
The content frame pathname, unchanged across docking transitions\&.
.PP
.RS -4n
\fBinsert\fR (::splitlayout::splitlayout)
.RE
.PP
Inserts an empty leaf beside an existing non-root node\&.
.RS 4n
\fBSPLITLAYOUTOBJ insert\fP \fInode ?-side side?\fP
.br
.RE
.PP
\fBParameters\fR
.RS 4n
.TP
-side side
Position of the new leaf relative to node: before or after; default after\&.
.TP
node
Identifier of the leaf or split beside which to insert a sibling\&.
.RE
.PP
\fBDescription\fR
.PP
The new leaf shares the target parent, and the target pane share is divided equally between the two siblings\&.
Inserting beside the root raises an error; split the root first\&.
.PP
\fBReturn value\fR
.PP
The new leaf identifier\&.
.PP
.RS -4n
\fBleaves\fR (::splitlayout::splitlayout)
.RE
.PP
Collects the leaves below a logical node\&.
.RS 4n
\fBSPLITLAYOUTOBJ leaves\fP \fI?id?\fP
.br
.RE
.PP
\fBParameters\fR
.RS 4n
.TP
id
Subtree root identifier; empty selects the layout root\&. Optional, default ""\&.
.RE
.PP
\fBDescription\fR
.PP
Traversal follows pane order recursively\&. A leaf includes itself\&.
.PP
\fBReturn value\fR
.PP
A list of leaf identifiers in depth-first pane order\&.
.PP
.RS -4n
\fBlocate\fR (::splitlayout::splitlayout)
.RE
.PP
Finds the leaf containing an application widget\&.
.RS 4n
\fBSPLITLAYOUTOBJ locate\fP \fIpath\fP
.br
.RE
.PP
\fBParameters\fR
.RS 4n
.TP
path
Widget pathname to locate\&.
.RE
.PP
\fBDescription\fR
.PP
Walks the actual Tk parent chain looking for a managed content frame\&.
A split panedwindow does not identify a leaf\&.
.PP
\fBReturn value\fR
.PP
The containing leaf identifier, or empty for a missing or unrelated widget\&.
.PP
.RS -4n
\fBminsize\fR (::splitlayout::splitlayout)
.RE
.PP
Gets or sets the minimum content-area dimensions of a leaf or floating window\&.
.RS 4n
\fBSPLITLAYOUTOBJ minsize\fP \fIid ?width height?\fP
.br
.RE
.PP
\fBParameters\fR
.RS 4n
.TP
height
Optional nonnegative integer minimum content height; must accompany width\&.
.TP
id
Leaf identifier or owned floating token; splits cannot own content minima\&.
.TP
width
Optional nonnegative integer minimum content width in pixels\&.
.RE
.PP
\fBDescription\fR
.PP
Defaults to {0 0}\&. The values follow content through retained splits, swaps, moves, and docking\&.
Header and border space is added by requiredsize\&. Validation is atomic; setting cancels active gestures\&.
.PP
\fBReturn value\fR
.PP
The content minimum as {width height}\&.
.PP
.RS -4n
\fBmove\fR (::splitlayout::splitlayout)
.RE
.PP
Moves an existing leaf to an edge or sibling position without destroying its content\&.
.RS 4n
\fBSPLITLAYOUTOBJ move\fP \fIleaf -left|-right|-top|-bottom|-before|-after target\fP
.br
.RE
.PP
\fBParameters\fR
.RS 4n
.TP
-after target
Insert after a non-root leaf or split in its existing parent\&.
.TP
-before target
Insert before a non-root leaf or split in its existing parent\&.
.TP
-bottom target
Place after a target leaf or split in a vertical split\&.
.TP
-left target
Place before a target leaf or split in a horizontal split\&.
.TP
-right target
Place after a target leaf or split in a horizontal split\&.
.TP
-top target
Place before a target leaf or split in a vertical split\&.
.TP
leaf
Existing source leaf identifier, retained by the move\&.
.RE
.PP
\fBDescription\fR
.PP
Exactly one destination option is required\&. Edge destinations reuse the target parent when its orientation
matches\&. Otherwise the target leaf becomes a split, and its existing frame moves to a newly identified leaf,
following split's identifier semantics\&. A split target is instead wrapped in a newly identified split,
preserving the target subtree; normal collapse may subsequently promote its sole remaining child\&. A split
target may contain the source, including the root\&. The source identifier, frame, widget paths, and state
survive\&.
.PP
Within one parent and orientation, panes are reordered with their existing weights\&. Other moves divide the
target share equally between the source and target; remaining source siblings retain relative proportions\&.
A source parent left with one child is collapsed after insertion\&. Moving to oneself or to an already adjacent
position does nothing\&. Argument errors are detected before changing layout or cancelling a deferred drag\&.
A successful structural move cancels deferred dragging and rebuilds geometry once, without nested updates\&.
.PP
\fBReturn value\fR
.PP
The unchanged source leaf identifier\&. Query parent, children, or tree for the resulting structure\&.
.PP
.RS -4n
\fBnode\fR (::splitlayout::splitlayout)
.RE
.PP
Finds the logical node associated with an exact widget pathname\&.
.RS 4n
\fBSPLITLAYOUTOBJ node\fP \fIpath\fP
.br
.RE
.PP
\fBParameters\fR
.RS 4n
.TP
path
Managed content frame, pane wrapper, or panedwindow pathname\&.
.RE
.PP
\fBDescription\fR
.PP
This method does not search ancestors; use locate for application widgets inside a content frame\&.
.PP
\fBReturn value\fR
.PP
The node identifier, or an empty string when no node matches\&.
.PP
.RS -4n
\fBpanedwindow\fR (::splitlayout::splitlayout)
.RE
.PP
Returns the native panedwindow of a split, independently of structure visibility\&.
The native ttk panedwindow pathname\&. Leaf identifiers raise an error\&.
.RS 4n
\fBSPLITLAYOUTOBJ panedwindow\fP \fIid\fP
.br
.RE
.PP
\fBParameters\fR
.RS 4n
.TP
id
Existing split identifier\&.
.RE
.PP
\fBReturn value\fR
.PP
Returns the native panedwindow of a split, independently of structure visibility\&.
The native ttk panedwindow pathname\&. Leaf identifiers raise an error\&.
.PP
.RS -4n
\fBparent\fR (::splitlayout::splitlayout)
.RE
.PP
Returns the logical parent of a node\&.
The parent node identifier, or an empty string for the root\&.
.RS 4n
\fBSPLITLAYOUTOBJ parent\fP \fIid\fP
.br
.RE
.PP
\fBParameters\fR
.RS 4n
.TP
id
Existing logical node identifier\&.
.RE
.PP
\fBDescription\fR
.PP
This relationship is independent of the actual Tk widget parent\&.
.PP
\fBReturn value\fR
.PP
Returns the logical parent of a node\&.
The parent node identifier, or an empty string for the root\&.
.PP
.RS -4n
\fBproportions\fR (::splitlayout::splitlayout)
.RE
.PP
Queries or sets all pane proportions of a split\&.
.RS 4n
\fBSPLITLAYOUTOBJ proportions\fP \fInode ?weights?\fP
.br
.RE
.PP
\fBParameters\fR
.RS 4n
.TP
node
Identifier of an existing split\&.
.TP
weights
Optional list containing one finite positive weight per child, in pane order\&. Omit to query\&.
.RE
.PP
\fBDescription\fR
.PP
Weights are normalized before storage\&. Setting cancels any active deferred drag and schedules geometry
application at idle\&. A query captures current sash positions when geometry is usable and no application is
pending; otherwise it returns the stored proportions\&.
.PP
\fBReturn value\fR
.PP
The normalized list of pane proportions\&.
.PP
.RS -4n
\fBratio\fR (::splitlayout::splitlayout)
.RE
.PP
Queries or sets the first pane fraction of a two-child split\&.
.RS 4n
\fBSPLITLAYOUTOBJ ratio\fP \fInode ?value?\fP
.br
.RE
.PP
\fBParameters\fR
.RS 4n
.TP
node
Identifier of a split with exactly two children\&.
.TP
value
Optional finite first-pane fraction strictly between zero and one\&. Omit to query\&.
.RE
.PP
\fBDescription\fR
.PP
Use proportions for splits with more children\&. Setting stores the fraction and its complement, cancels a
deferred drag, and schedules idle geometry application\&. Querying captures current geometry when possible,
using the same conditions as proportions\&.
.PP
\fBReturn value\fR
.PP
The stored first pane fraction\&.
.PP
.RS -4n
\fBremove\fR (::splitlayout::splitlayout)
.RE
.PP
Removes a docked subtree or destroys owned floating content\&.
.RS 4n
\fBSPLITLAYOUTOBJ remove\fP \fIid\fP
.br
.RE
.PP
\fBParameters\fR
.RS 4n
.TP
id
Existing node identifier or floating token to remove\&.
.RE
.PP
\fBDescription\fR
.PP
Removing the root behaves like clear\&. Otherwise, the parent retains its remaining children with normalized
weights\&. If only one child remains, that child replaces the parent split\&. Surviving content is preserved\&.
.PP
\fBReturn value\fR
.PP
The surviving parent split, its promoted child, or new root; empty for floating removal\&.
.PP
.RS -4n
\fBrequiredsize\fR (::splitlayout::splitlayout)
.RE
.PP
Calculates a node's minimum outer size, including manager-owned decoration\&.
.RS 4n
\fBSPLITLAYOUTOBJ requiredsize\fP \fIid\fP
.br
.RE
.PP
\fBParameters\fR
.RS 4n
.TP
id
Leaf, split, or owned floating token\&.
.RE
.PP
\fBDescription\fR
.PP
Splits sum child minima and themed sashes along their orientation, and take the maximum across it\&.
Includes header/button space, leaf borders, and split outlines/padding\&. Does not include hull padding,
native window decorations, or application widget requests\&. This query does not resize the owner window\&.
.PP
\fBReturn value\fR
.PP
A two-element {width height} list in pixels\&.
.PP
.RS -4n
\fBretain\fR (::splitlayout::splitlayout)
.RE
.PP
Keeps a node while removing its siblings and collapsing its parent\&.
.RS 4n
\fBSPLITLAYOUTOBJ retain\fP \fIid\fP
.br
.RE
.PP
\fBParameters\fR
.RS 4n
.TP
id
Existing node identifier to retain\&.
.RE
.PP
\fBDescription\fR
.PP
Destroys all sibling subtrees and their content, then promotes the retained node in place of its immediate
parent\&. Only that parent level is collapsed\&. Retaining the root makes no changes\&.
.PP
\fBReturn value\fR
.PP
The retained node identifier\&.
.PP
.RS -4n
\fBroot\fR (::splitlayout::splitlayout)
.RE
.PP
Returns the current logical root node\&.
The root node identifier\&.
.RS 4n
\fBSPLITLAYOUTOBJ root\fP
.br
.RE
.PP
\fBReturn value\fR
.PP
Returns the current logical root node\&.
The root node identifier\&.
.PP
.RS -4n
\fBsplit\fR (::splitlayout::splitlayout)
.RE
.PP
Replaces a leaf with a split containing two new leaves\&.
.RS 4n
\fBSPLITLAYOUTOBJ split\fP \fInode ?-orient orientation? ?-ratio fraction? ?-keep child?\fP
.br
.RE
.PP
\fBParameters\fR
.RS 4n
.TP
-keep child
Child receiving the existing content: first or second; default first\&.
.TP
-orient orientation
Split orientation: horizontal or vertical; default horizontal\&.
.TP
-ratio fraction
First child fraction, strictly between zero and one; default 0\&.5\&.
.TP
node
Identifier of the existing leaf to split\&.
.RE
.PP
\fBDescription\fR
.PP
The original leaf identifier becomes the split identifier, and both child leaves receive new identifiers\&.
The retained content frame and its widget descendants are reused without reparenting or recreation\&. The other
child starts with an empty frame\&. Existing pane proportions are captured before rebuilding the layout\&.
.PP
\fBReturn value\fR
.PP
The two new leaf identifiers in pane order\&.
.PP
.RS -4n
\fBswap\fR (::splitlayout::splitlayout)
.RE
.PP
Exchanges the content frames assigned to two leaves\&.
.RS 4n
\fBSPLITLAYOUTOBJ swap\fP \fIa b\fP
.br
.RE
.PP
\fBParameters\fR
.RS 4n
.TP
a
First leaf identifier\&.
.TP
b
Second leaf identifier\&.
.RE
.PP
\fBDescription\fR
.PP
The leaves can belong to different splits and nesting levels\&. Their logical positions remain unchanged, while
complete content frames and their existing widget state move between those positions\&. Actual Tk parentage and
widget pathnames remain unchanged\&. Equal identifiers do nothing\&.
.PP
\fBReturn value\fR
.PP
Nothing\&.
.PP
.RS -4n
\fBtitle\fR (::splitlayout::splitlayout)
.RE
.PP
Gets or sets the optional header title belonging to managed content\&.
.RS 4n
\fBSPLITLAYOUTOBJ title\fP \fIid ?text?\fP
.br
.RE
.PP
\fBParameters\fR
.RS 4n
.TP
id
Docked leaf identifier or owned floating token\&.
.TP
text
Optional title; an empty string removes the heading\&.
.RE
.PP
\fBDescription\fR
.PP
Titles follow content through swaps, moves, splitting with retained content, and floating transitions\&.
They do not change the native window title set by undock -title\&. Rendering uses one line and truncates
long headings to keep the buttons unobscured; the stored string is returned unchanged\&.
.PP
\fBReturn value\fR
.PP
The current title string\&.
.PP
.RS -4n
\fBtree\fR (::splitlayout::splitlayout)
.RE
.PP
Returns a recursive snapshot of the logical layout hierarchy\&.
A nested dictionary describing the requested logical subtree\&.
.RS 4n
\fBSPLITLAYOUTOBJ tree\fP \fI?id?\fP
.br
.RE
.PP
\fBParameters\fR
.RS 4n
.TP
id
Subtree root identifier; empty selects the layout root\&. Optional, default ""\&.
.RE
.PP
\fBDescription\fR
.PP
Each node dictionary contains id, type, parent, widget, and children\&. With handles enabled, widget is the
wrapper for a leaf\&. Leaf dictionaries also contain the application content frame as frame,
with an empty children list\&. Split dictionaries contain orient and proportions; two-child splits also contain
ratio\&. With showstructure, widget is the enclosing split wrapper\&. The panedwindow field always names
the native split widget\&. Split children are nested node dictionaries in pane order\&.
.PP
Proportions are refreshed from usable geometry unless application is pending\&.
.PP
\fBReturn value\fR
.PP
Returns a recursive snapshot of the logical layout hierarchy\&.
A nested dictionary describing the requested logical subtree\&.
.PP
.RS -4n
\fBtype\fR (::splitlayout::splitlayout)
.RE
.PP
Returns the type of a logical node\&.
The string leaf or split\&.
.RS 4n
\fBSPLITLAYOUTOBJ type\fP \fIid\fP
.br
.RE
.PP
\fBParameters\fR
.RS 4n
.TP
id
Existing logical node identifier\&.
.RE
.PP
\fBReturn value\fR
.PP
Returns the type of a logical node\&.
The string leaf or split\&.
.PP
.RS -4n
\fBundock\fR (::splitlayout::splitlayout)
.RE
.PP
Removes a leaf from the layout and promotes its retained content to a floating window\&.
.RS 4n
\fBSPLITLAYOUTOBJ undock\fP \fIleaf ?-title title? ?-geometry geometry? ?-onclose action?\fP
.br
.RE
.PP
\fBParameters\fR
.RS 4n
.TP
-geometry geometry
Optional Tk window geometry; defaults to the current content size and screen position\&.
.TP
-onclose action
Window close action: dock or destroy; default dock\&.
.TP
-title title
Window title; default splitlayout\&.
.TP
leaf
Existing docked leaf identifier\&.
.RE
.PP
\fBDescription\fR
.PP
Content widgets and their pathnames survive\&. The former leaf identifier expires and single-child parents
collapse\&. Undocking the sole root leaves an empty placeholder leaf\&. Only this manager's content is accepted\&.
The floating frame keeps its actual parent, so destroying the hull also destroys its floating windows\&.
.PP
\fBReturn value\fR
.PP
An opaque floating token, accepted by dock, floating, frame, and remove\&.
.PP
.RS -4n
\fBwidget\fR (::splitlayout::splitlayout)
.RE
.PP
Returns the widget associated with a logical node\&.
The outer geometry widget: a leaf wrapper with drag handles, a split wrapper with showstructure,
or the content frame/native panedwindow otherwise\&. Use panedwindow to access a split's native widget\&.
.RS 4n
\fBSPLITLAYOUTOBJ widget\fP \fIid\fP
.br
.RE
.PP
\fBParameters\fR
.RS 4n
.TP
id
Existing logical node identifier\&.
.RE
.PP
\fBReturn value\fR
.PP
Returns the widget associated with a logical node\&.
The outer geometry widget: a leaf wrapper with drag handles, a split wrapper with showstructure,
or the content frame/native panedwindow otherwise\&. Use panedwindow to access a split's native widget\&.
.PP
.RS -4n
\fBwidgets\fR (::splitlayout::splitlayout)
.RE
.PP
Returns a recursive snapshot of the actual Tk widget hierarchy\&.
A nested widget dictionary with path, parent, class, manager, container, and children\&.
.RS 4n
\fBSPLITLAYOUTOBJ widgets\fP \fI?path?\fP
.br
.RE
.PP
\fBParameters\fR
.RS 4n
.TP
path
Widget subtree root; empty selects the layout hull\&. Optional, default ""\&.
.RE
.PP
\fBDescription\fR
.PP
The pathname must exist and be the hull or one of its Tk descendants\&. This hierarchy includes application
widgets and is independent of the logical tree returned by tree\&.
.PP
\fBReturn value\fR
.PP
Returns a recursive snapshot of the actual Tk widget hierarchy\&.
A nested widget dictionary with path, parent, class, manager, container, and children\&.


.SH SEE ALSO
splitlayout(n) - package's main page
.br
.sp 1
Public commands and classes documentation:
.br
splitlayout-splitlayout(n)